# module Responses handler boundary

Owns the complete Responses route after routing: body parsing, normalization,
previous-response ownership, caller-tool validation/suspension, ACP turn execution,
canonical stream finalization, storage admission, response shaping and release.
Shared sink/session accounting primitives come from `turn-runtime.js`; timer and
disconnect ownership comes from `turn-lifecycle.js`. `server.js` must not duplicate
this handler.

# invariant Refuse before continuation claim

Malformed or unsupported parameters and unknown models must fail without claiming
or refreshing any retained Responses conversation.

# invariant Advancement invalidates an unsuccessful continuation

Once a prompt is sent or a matching tool result is resolved, a failure, timeout or
disconnect closes the chain while preserving prior GET snapshots. Pre-turn
validation leaves the claimed tip usable. Unknown, duplicate, standalone or mixed
tool outputs fail before result resolution or prompt spawn.

`Agent.preflightTurn` validates prompt capabilities and request-level options before
the handler marks the turn advanced. A recoverable 400 therefore releases the
claim without closing the chain; failure after the subsequent `agent.turn` call is
treated as an attempted ACP turn and invalidates it.

# invariant One HTTP owner per pending Responses turn

Claiming a suspended Responses continuation atomically marks its pending turn
attached and returns an opaque claim token. A concurrent result request receives
409 `conversation_busy` before calling `ToolBridge.resolve`; it may neither detach
the first owner nor invalidate that owner's conversation. Releasing the token
keeps a still-pending conversation busy while making it adoptable again.

# invariant Streaming finalizes before storage

`ResponseStream.finalize` produces the canonical immutable response before
`SessionStore.record` performs byte admission. The completed SSE event and GET use
that same finalized value; no stream step may mutate it after accounting.
